meta {
  name: Local Payment
  seq: 1
}

auth {
  mode: inherit
}

docs {
  A local payment (sender and receiver on the same node) driven end to end
  the way an Open Payments client drives it: every request is signed, and
  every resource request carries a GNAP access token.

  Setup (Admin) → discover wallet addresses → incoming payment grant +
  incoming payment → quote grant + quote → interactive outgoing payment
  grant → start / accept / finish interaction → continue grant → outgoing
  payment → Verify.

  Run the whole folder against the `Rhyza` environment (`pnpm docker:up`).

  Client identity:
  - The incoming payment and quote grants use directed identity
    (`client.jwk`), signing with the key from `scripts.generateClientKeys()`.
  - The outgoing payment grant needs interaction, and the spec (and auth
    domain) reject `client.jwk` for interactive grants. So it identifies the
    client by the sender wallet address instead, and Setup registers the same
    public key on that wallet address so the auth server finds it in
    `/jwks.json`.

  Request signing: every grant and resource request (not the Admin API or the
  IDP interaction calls) runs `scripts.addSignatureHeaders()` as its
  pre-request script. That:
  - generates an Ed25519 key pair on first use and keeps it in the runtime
    vars `clientPrivateKey` / `clientPublicJwk` / `clientKeyId`
    (`kid: bruno-client-key`), so one key covers the whole run;
  - resolves `{{...}}` variables in the URL, headers and body *before*
    signing and writes the resolved body back, so the signature covers the
    bytes actually sent;
  - POSTs the request to `httpSignatureServiceUrl`
    (`http-signature-service`, port 3010), which signs it with
    `@interledger/http-signature-utils` and returns `Signature`, `Signature-Input` and
    `Content-Digest` (SHA-512);
  - sets those headers on the outgoing request.

  Bruno never does the crypto itself. Note that `keyid` in `Signature-Input`
  is always `bruno-client-key`, so for the outgoing payment grant the key
  Setup publishes at `<senderWalletAddress>/jwks.json` must carry that exact
  `kid` or verification fails.

  Interaction: there is no IDP UI locally, so Bruno plays the user agent. It
  stops at each redirect, carries the `interactionSessionId` session cookie
  from `/start` to `/finish` itself, and accepts the grant by calling
  `idp-interaction-service` directly.
}
